 them  there was nothing clever or witty about  Khrushchev he was coarse and direct the  Urbane American president had little  experience dealing with men as crude and  outspoken as cruise ship who didn't have  much respect for anyone he didn't fear  he would have tried to intimidate any  adversary whether it happened to be  Kennedy or a Republican president he  hated Richard Nixon with whom he had  famously debated at a U.S exhibition in  Moscow in 1959. as he later complained  to an American visitor quote Mr Nixon  reminds me of a herring salesman who  would sell spoiled Herring as the real  thing  at the end of The Summit Kennedy thought  that his efforts were a complete failure  that the Soviet leader had treated him  with contempt and was determined to  cause him more trouble in the months  ahead  I've got two problems he confided  privately afterward first to figure out  why he did it and in such a hostile way  and second to figure out what we can do  about it  I think the first part is pretty easy to  explain I think he did it because of the  Bay of Pigs I think he thought that  anyone who was so young and  inexperienced as to get into that mess  could be taken and anyone who got into  it and didn't see it through  had no guts  so he just beat the hell out of me  other observers were not so pessimistic  about Kennedy's performance they thought  that he had held his ground well and  that Khrushchev was more than a little  impressed by the young president's  toughness and intelligence  in an interview given to the Kennedy  Library in 1966 the American ambassador  to the Kremlin at the time of the summit  Llewellyn Thompson recalled that  Khrushchev quote made quite clear that  he in a way envied the president at that  time it looked as though Kennedy had  many years ahead of him he envied his  Youth and as I say I think he quite  respected his ability and his grasp of  these questions  President Kennedy had spent a lot of  time discussing with me and others  Soviet Affairs and he had done a lot of  reading and was I think quite  knowledgeable  I imagine this impressed Khrushchev  but whatever favorable impression he may  have had of the younger leader the old  veteran of the Soviet state was still  going to make trouble whenever and  wherever he might testing Kennedy's  resolve and trying to get away with as  much as he could  in fact it took the Communists little  more than two months after the Vienna  Summit to launch their most provocative  plan for Berlin dividing the city with a  concrete barrier between the East and  West sections  the Berlin Wall as it came to be known  was harder on the people in the  Communist Zone because it was meant to  stop the loss of brains and talent  fleeing to freedom in the West  by some estimates more than two and a  half million people had left East Berlin  in just a 12-year period leading up to  1961.  the wall trapped the remaining  population and quickly became the most  reviled symbol of Oppression during the  Cold War  indeed in the long term building the  wall was a greater blunder for the  Communists than the Bay of Pigs was for  the U.S at least 130 people were killed  trying to escape over the wall and the  Damage Done to the international image  of the Communist cause  was devastating  the failure at the Vienna Summit was not  Kennedy's but khrushchevs who  overestimated his own position and who  couldn't resist the chance to undermine  this new leader in the West  although it was alarming in its brutal  appearance building the Berlin Wall was  itself a sign of weakness on the Soviet  side  a recognition not only of their failures  to Rally East berliners to the Communist  cause  but also I Surrender of sorts to the  West  how so before construction of the wall  began on August the 13th 1961.  the overwhelming worry in America was  that the Soviets would invade West  Berlin and eliminate it as a Haven of  Freedom inside East Germany  instead they built the wall which is bad 