edy had long harbored  doubts about the value of the Space  Program  but Shepherd's success changed  everything  such was the relief when his capsule  returned safely to Earth that the  astronaut was treated to an enormous  outpouring of adulation by the American  public  the President also gave him a hero's  welcome at the White House  it was hard to imagine how this first  foray into space would result in a moon  landing for American astronauts in just  eight years  but that's what John Kennedy promised  only 20 days after Alan Shepard's flight  addressing a special session of Congress  the president made one of his most  famous pronouncements when he declared  quote I believe that this nation should  commit itself to achieving the goal  before this decade is out of Landing a  man on the moon and returning him safely  to the Earth  amazingly in the film of his speech we  can see that the members of Congress  greeted the statement with little  enthusiasm so Fantastical did the notion  of going to the Moon seem in those days  there is just a brief sound of a few  Representatives starting to clap and  then silence  my guess is that a lot of those  politicians thought that they were  listening to a wild campaign promise  that would never be fulfilled in their  lifetimes  which is why the speech is so thrilling  to watch now  we know that Kennedy's promise was  fulfilled and we know that it sparked a  huge technological Revolution that not  only changed our relationship to space  but also our lives on this planet  we cannot overestimate the profound  impact of Kennedy's challenge especially  when we consider how outrageously  ambitious it seemed in 1961.  he was taken advantage of a moment in  time to launch a massive change that  would influence events for decades to  come which is surely the kind of Grand  Vision we expect from a great leader  whatever other faults that leader may  have  after the embarrassing setback in Cuba  the previous month Kennedy wanted a  reset for his presidency  he had his eye on big changes and wanted  to avoid if at all possible getting  bogged down in Endless conflicts or  Petty disputes over small things  the basic dynamic in his presidency was  the ever-present struggle between his  cautious side and his desire to go big  and bold  with his speech on May 25th he went so  big and so bold that he left many in his  own party bewildered  as for the Republicans many of them  thought he was being hopelessly  unrealistic and quickly seized on the  predictable charge that he was proposing  billions for quote Pie in the Sky  spending as the Republican National  chairman Senator thruston Morton of  Kentucky put it in the New York Times  in purely political terms it just didn't  make much sense for Kennedy to go so far  out on a limb and promise the Moon  in the short term it didn't change much  at home or abroad and the Soviets didn't  seem any more impressed by his Moon talk  than the Republicans were  but it made all the difference to his  legacy that he made this promise  it was rooted both in his faith in  America's technological potential and in  his recognition that the times demanded  action that would result in a surge of  progress that could transform the nation  and the world  you don't get much more forward-looking  than Kennedy does In this lesser-known  passage from his speech to Congress  now is the time to take longer strides  time for a great new American Enterprise  time for this nation to take a clearly  leading role in space achievement which  in many ways May hold the key to our  future on Earth  promising the moon was the greatest  moment of Kennedy's presidency it was  transformative enduring and politically  courageous perhaps even more impressive  it was unexpected but the rocky start  that marked his administration in the  first half of 1961 and the devious  intrigues of both Kennedy's own  intelligence community and the Soviets  gave him the impetus to try something  radically dif