 them  there was nothing clever or witty about  Khrushchev he was coarse and direct the  Urbane American president had little  experience dealing with men as crude and  outspoken as cruise ship who didn't have  much respect for anyone he didn't fear  he would have tried to intimidate any  adversary whether it happened to be  Kennedy or a Republican president he  hated Richard Nixon with whom he had  famously debated at a U.S exhibition in  Moscow in 1959. as he later complained  to an American visitor quote Mr Nixon  reminds me of a herring salesman who  would sell spoiled Herring as the real  thing  at the end of The Summit Kennedy thought  that his efforts were a complete failure  that the Soviet leader had treated him  with contempt and was determined to  cause him more trouble in the months  ahead  I've got two problems he confided  privately afterward first to figure out  why he did it and in such a hostile way  and second to figure out what we can do  about it  I think the first part is pretty easy to  explain I think he did it because of the  Bay of Pigs I think he thought that  anyone who was so young and  inexperienced as to get into that mess  could be taken and anyone who got into  it and didn't see it through  had no guts  so he just beat the hell out of me  other observers were not so pessimistic  about Kennedy's performance they thought  that he had held his ground well and  that Khrushchev was more than a little  impressed by the young president's  toughness and intelligence  in an interview given to the Kennedy  Library in 1966 the American ambassador  to the Kremlin at the time of the summit  Llewellyn Thompson recalled that  Khrushchev quote made quite clear that  he in a way envied the president at that  time it looked as though Kennedy had  many years ahead of him he envied his  Youth and as I say I think he quite  respected his ability and his grasp of  these questions  President Kennedy had spent a lot of  time discussing with me and others  Soviet Affairs and he had done a lot of  reading and was I think quite  knowledgeable  I imagine this impressed Khrushchev  but whatever favorable impression he may  have had of the younger leader the old  veteran of the Soviet state was still  going to make trouble whenever and  wherever he might testing Kennedy's  resolve and trying to get away with as  much as he could  in fact it took the Communists little  more than two months after the Vienna  Summit to launch their most provocative  plan for Berlin dividing the city with a  concrete barrier between the East and  West sections  the Berlin Wall as it came to be known  was harder on the people in the  Communist Zone because it was meant to  stop the loss of brains and talent  fleeing to freedom in the West  by some estimates more than two and a  half million people had left East Berlin  in just a 12-year period leading up to  1961.  the wall trapped the remaining  population and quickly became the most  reviled symbol of Oppression during the  Cold War  indeed in the long term building the  wall was a greater blunder for the  Communists than the Bay of Pigs was for  the U.S at least 130 people were killed  trying to escape over the wall and the  Damage Done to the international image  of the Communist cause  was devastating  the failure at the Vienna Summit was not  Kennedy's but khrushchevs who  overestimated his own position and who  couldn't resist the chance to undermine  this new leader in the West  although it was alarming in its brutal  appearance building the Berlin Wall was  itself a sign of weakness on the Soviet  side  a recognition not only of their failures  to Rally East berliners to the Communist  cause  but also I Surrender of sorts to the  West  how so before construction of the wall  began on August the 13th 1961.  the overwhelming worry in America was  that the Soviets would invade West  Berlin and eliminate it as a Haven of  Freedom inside East Germany  instead they built the wall which is bad  enough for the people living behind it  but it was not a direct assault on West  Berlin  if the Soviets had ever been tempted to  launch such an assault  John Kennedy took a dramatic step to  dissuade them in a nationally televised  address to the American people the  previous month on July 25th  he looked tired and pale on television  and was under great strain not only  because of the international tensions  but also because he had suffered a  setback in his long struggle with his  health shortly after his return from  Vienna he was on crutches again taking  more medications that were good for him  to control the pain in his back  but his intense somber expression at  times during the address gave his words  more weight he was deadly serious about  stopping any invasion of West Berlin and  made it clear that he would retaliate  against such an action with all the  force at his command  these are the words from Kennedy that  Khrushchev could not afford to ignore  we do not want to fight but we have  fought before  those who threaten to unleash the forces  of War on a dispute over West Berlin  should recall the words of the ancient  philosopher a man who causes fear cannot  be free from Fear  in other words Mr Khrushchev it's time  to stop pushing your luck and pull back  before it's too late  that was the kind of message the Kremlin  understood  three weeks later the wall went up as a  face-saving tactic for the belligerent  Soviets a provocative act designed to  look aggressive without unleashing as  Kennedy put it the forces of War  the two leaders would soon lock horns  again during the worst episode of the  Cold War the Cuban Missile Crisis of  October 1962 where misunderstandings on  both sides would bring the world to the  brink of nuclear war  we will study the frightening Arc of  that crisis in the next lecture but for  now it's important to point out that  instead of just rattling his sword candy  also had less confrontational responses  to the Communist challenge in his  Arsenal  he was prepared to do all the usual  things to build up defenses and resist  aggression  but he also had the imagination to see  that the West  could counteract the Soviet threat in  less dangerous ways than say a military  confrontation over Berlin  in keeping with the aspirational tone of  his inaugural address  he wanted to showcase the best of  America in contrast to the worst  excesses of Soviet tyranny what he  offered first was the Peace Corps an  unarmed Army of volunteers willing to  show how modern America could offer a  helping hand to the rest of the world  his second step was to demonstrate the  full measure of American ingenuity  by starting a race to reach the Moon by  the end of the decade  which enabled competition between the  two superpowers  but without the threat of War  it seemed an impossible goal at the time  and many people thought it was a wasted  effort what was the point they asked  what was a moon landing supposed to  prove  NASA had been set up in the Eisenhower  Administration to explore space  technology but Eisenhower himself  believed that sending astronauts to the  Moon would only be a quote stunt saying  that he quote couldn't care less whether  a man ever reached the Moon  in any case America lagged behind the  Soviets when the Space Race began  first in October 1957 when Sputnik 1 was  launched then again in April 1961 when  Cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin had become the  first human in outer space orbiting the  Earth in his capsule lostock won  the best that the United States could do  in response was to launch its own  satellite into space in 1958. then send  astronaut Alan Shepard on a brief  sub-orbital space flight on May the 5th  1961. it was a major achievement for the  American space program but in  photographs taken at the White House  when the launch was televised  you can see the apprehension on the  faces of Kennedy and his staff  they look anything but confident in  those pictures  and in fact Kennedy had long harbored  doubts about the value of the Space  Program  but Shepherd's success changed  everything  such was the relief when his capsule  returned safely to Earth that the  astronaut was treated to an enormous  outpouring of adulation by the American  public  the President also gave him a hero's  welcome at the White House  it was hard to imagine how this first  foray into space would result in a moon  landing for American astronauts in just  eight years  but that's what John Kennedy promised  only 20 days after Alan Shepard's flight  addressing a special session of Congress  the president made one of his most  famous pronouncements when he declared  quote I believe that this nation should  commit itself to achieving the goal  before this decade is out of Landing a  man on the moon and returning him safely  to the Earth  amazingly in the film of his speech we  can see that the members of Congress  greeted the statement with little  enthusiasm so Fantastical did the notion  of going to the Moon seem in those days  there is just a brief sound of a few  Representatives starting to clap and  then silence  my guess is that a lot of those  politicians thought that they were  listening to a wild campaign promise  that would never be fulfilled in their  lifetimes  which is why the speech is so thrilling  to watch now  we know that Kennedy's promise was  fulfilled and we know that it sparked a  huge technological Revolution that not  only changed our relationship to space  but also our lives on this planet  we cannot overestimate the profound  impact of Kennedy's challenge especially  when we consider how outrageously  ambitious it seemed in 1961.  he was taken advantage of a moment in  time to launch a massive change that  would influence events for decades to  come which is surely the kind of Grand  Vision we expect from a great leader  whatever other faults that leader may  have  after the embarrassing setback in Cuba  the previous month Kennedy wanted a  reset for his presidency  he had his eye on big changes and wanted  to avoid if at all possible getting  bogged down in Endless conflicts or  Petty disputes over small things  the basic dynamic in his presidency was  the ever-present struggle between his  cautious side and his desire to go big  and bold  with his speech on May 25th he went so  big and so bold that he left many in his  own party bewildered  as for the Republicans many of them  thought he was being hopelessly  unrealistic and quickly seized on the  predictable charge that he was proposing  billions for quote Pie in the Sky  spending as the Republican National  chairman Senator thruston Morton of  Kentucky put it in the New York Times  in purely political terms it just didn't  make much sense for Kennedy to go so far  out on a limb and promise the Moon  in the short term it didn't change much  at home or abroad and the Soviets didn't  seem any more impressed by his Moon talk  than the Republicans were  but it made all the difference to his  legacy that he made this promise  it was rooted both in his faith in  America's technological potential and in  his recognition that the times demanded  action that would result in a surge of  progress that could transform the nation  and the world  you don't get much more forward-looking  than Kennedy does In this lesser-known  passage from his speech to Congress  now is the time to take longer strides  time for a great new American Enterprise  time for this nation to take a clearly  leading role in space achievement which  in many ways May hold the key to our  future on Earth  promising the moon was the greatest  moment of Kennedy's presidency it was  transformative enduring and politically  courageous perhaps even more impressive  it was unexpected but the rocky start  that marked his administration in the  first half of 1961 and the devious  intrigues of both Kennedy's own  intelligence community and the Soviets  gave him the impetus to try something  radically different  which is another sign of great  leadership the willingness to change  course and Forge A New Path  it also allowed Kennedy to find a better  way of employing his vice president  on April 20th only a few days after Yuri  gagarin's space flight  Kennedy sent an Urgent Message to Lyndon  Johnson about the potential for  accelerating and expanding America's  Space Program  why did he go to Johnson for this  when he was a majority leader everyone  in the Senate knew that Johnson was  passionate about space exploration he  had helped to set up a National Space  Council in the late 1950s and JFK had  made him its chairman one of only two  committees he had been assigned to by  the new president  eager to have something substantial to  occupy him as vice president  Johnson would make the Space Program his  special area of interest and he began in  April by urging Kennedy to pursue a  moonshot  always competitive in nature Johnson Saw  the space race as a way to beat the  Soviets without having to fight them  in the eyes of the world he said first  in space means first period second in  space is second in everything  perhaps the most important Johnson knew  how to get the money from Congress to  fund an ambitious Space Program he  understood that a large Hall of cash for  projects in southern states would be  irresistible to his closest Senate  colleagues which helps to explain how so  much of the program ended up operating  out of places like Houston and  Huntsville Alabama home of NASA's  Marshall space flight center  after Alan Shepard's successful flight  candy couldn't resist TC Johnson quote  nobody knows that the vice president is  the chairman of the space Council but if  that flight had been a flop I guarantee  you that everybody would have known that  you were the chairman  overhearing the Joe Newton Meadow the  head of the Federal Communications  Commission added Mr president if the  flight had been a flop the vice  president would have been the next  astronaut  only a week after giving his speech on  May 25th Kennedy was on his way to meet  with Khrushchev in Vienna he was in the  middle of some of the most demanding  months of any presidency and he was  facing obstacles in all directions  before and end domestic it is no wonder  that his health took a turn for the  worst that summer he was besieged by  difficulties of extraordinary  proportions and the American public had  no idea of how precarious his health was  he made a recovery of course but there  were even greater challenges ahead and  he would need all his strength to  confront them as we shall see in our  next lecture on the pivotal year of  1962.
 

